Q1. What is Continuous Delivery (CD)? Explain how it works.

Interview Ready Answer

Continuous Delivery (CD) is a DevOps practice in which every successful
build produced by the Continuous Integration (CI) pipeline is
automatically prepared for release. The application is always kept in a
deployable state, but deployment to the production environment requires
manual approval.

Definition

Continuous Delivery is the practice of automatically preparing software
for release after Continuous Integration, while keeping manual control
over production deployment.

Purpose

• Keep the application release-ready at all times.
• Reduce deployment risk.
• Improve software quality.
• Deliver features faster.
• Allow controlled production releases.
• Enable quick rollback if required.

======================================================================

Q2. Explain the workflow of Continuous Delivery.

Interview Ready Answer

After the CI pipeline successfully builds and tests the application, the
Continuous Delivery pipeline packages the application and prepares it
for deployment. The application remains in a release-ready state until a
manual approval is given for deployment to the production environment.

CD Workflow

Developer Writes Code
        │
        ▼
Commit & Push
        │
        ▼
Continuous Integration
(Build + Test)
        │
        ▼
Package Application
        │
        ▼
Release Ready
        │
        ▼
Manual Approval
        │
        ▼
Deploy to Production

Important Point

Deployment to production is MANUAL in Continuous Delivery.

======================================================================

Q3. Why is Continuous Delivery considered safer than releasing software
only at the end of development?

Interview Ready Answer

Continuous Delivery is safer because software is delivered in small,
incremental, release-ready changes instead of one large release at the
end of development. Smaller changes are easier to test, debug, and roll
back if problems occur. Manual approval before production deployment
acts as an additional quality gate, reducing deployment risk.

Why CD is Safer

• Small and incremental releases.
• Easier testing.
• Faster bug detection.
• Easier rollback.
• Lower deployment risk.
• Manual approval before production deployment.
• Application is always release-ready.

Before Continuous Delivery

• Large releases.
• Difficult debugging.
• High deployment risk.
• Long release cycles.
• More production failures.

After Continuous Delivery

• Frequent releases.
• Faster testing.
• Faster recovery.
• Controlled deployment.
• Better software quality.

